# CONTRIBUTING

If you are reading this it means that you are probably interested in contributing to the language.
I am glad!

## How to contribute?

Feel free to create issues and discussions.

Contributions must be made through a comprehensive pull request to the `main`
branch (trunk-based; CI runs on `main` only).

## Coding conventions

Run these before pushing (CI enforces them):

- `cargo fmt --check` (or `cargo fmt` to fix)
- `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`
- `cargo check` / `cargo check --no-default-features`

No `rustfmt.toml`/`clippy.toml` — defaults are enforced by CI.

## Testing

- `cargo test` runs everything: in-tree unit tests plus `tests/e2e.rs`, which
  compiles every `samples/*.fib` through the library API and executes the
  result (exact-stdout asserts for the deterministic samples, `fib_bench`
  compile-only). It needs the default `llvm` feature and a C compiler.
- `cargo test --lib` runs only unit tests (also works with
  `--no-default-features`); `cargo test --test e2e` runs only the
  end-to-end suite.
- `cargo test --no-default-features` runs the frontend-only suite (no LLVM);
  `cargo test --all-features` is the full gate. Both run in CI.
- Conventions for new code:
  - Every new lowering branch ships with a `samples/*.fib` reproducer so e2e
    covers it.
  - Every soundness fix ships with a `get_typed_err` negative test.
  - For diagnostics, assert on `is_err()` plus a message substring/line —
    not the exact full message, which is still evolving.
- Coverage (`cargo tarpaulin`, advisory CI job, non-blocking): use it to
  find untested code, not to gate merges. Do not chase 100% line coverage
  on the lowering files — it incentivizes tautological tests.

## Commit messages

Branches are squash-merged with
[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) titles
(`feat(frontend): ...`, `fix(backend): ...`, `refactor(driver,cli): ...`,
`docs: ...`, `test: ...`). Keep PR titles merge-ready; single-word messages
(`idk`, `wip`) will be asked to be renamed.
